1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,196
This is Second Look: a calm second opinion for messages that feel off.

2
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:10,422
Scam texts and emails are built to make you act before you think.

3
00:00:10,772 --> 00:00:15,404
A fake delivery fee. A grandchild in trouble.

4
00:00:15,754 --> 00:00:19,930
The usual advice, be careful, doesn't help in the moment.

5
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:24,912
What helps is someone who reads the message with you, and points at the tricks.

6
00:00:25,860 --> 00:00:29,100
So you paste the message into Second Look.

7
00:00:29,450 --> 00:00:36,338
It runs entirely in your browser. There's no account, no server, and nothing is uploaded.

8
00:00:37,290 --> 00:00:40,578
Here's a typical package delivery text.

9
00:00:40,928 --> 00:00:44,864
Second Look says High risk, with four warning signs.

10
00:00:45,214 --> 00:00:51,838
It marks the exact words: a redelivery fee, within 24 hours, will be returned to sender.

11
00:00:52,188 --> 00:01:00,756
And it checks the link. The address starts with postal‑service.com, but the site you'd actually visit is help‑center.top.

12
00:01:01,710 --> 00:01:04,446
Now, the grandparent scam.

13
00:01:04,796 --> 00:01:08,228
Tap any marked phrase to see why it matters.

14
00:01:08,578 --> 00:01:14,698
Don't tell Mom. Scammers isolate you, because a relative would spot the trick.

15
00:01:15,048 --> 00:01:19,512
Gift cards, and send me the codes: money you can never get back.

16
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:27,612
Then it tells you what to do in the next five minutes, tailored to what it found, and always ending with what to do if you already paid.

17
00:01:27,962 --> 00:01:34,874
Ask someone I trust copies a ready-to-send note, with the verdict and the message, so a family member can take a look.

18
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:43,020
A normal message stays calm. Low risk, with an honest reminder that no tool is a guarantee.

19
00:01:43,970 --> 00:01:48,698
There's a larger text mode for anyone who needs it, and it works on a phone.

20
00:01:51,450 --> 00:01:57,906
I built Second Look for Build With AI: Basics, using the Devpost Skill Pack with an AI coding agent.

21
00:01:58,256 --> 00:02:08,552
Scope came first, and it cut the obvious idea of plugging in an AI model. That would cost money, send private messages to a third party, and couldn't show why.

22
00:02:08,902 --> 00:02:12,814
Instead: clear rules that point at the exact words.

23
00:02:13,164 --> 00:02:19,932
Then a PRD with acceptance criteria, a technical spec, and four thin slices, each tested and committed.

24
00:02:20,282 --> 00:02:24,722
Twenty automated tests include the messages that must stay calm.

25
00:02:25,670 --> 00:02:31,406
Second Look is free, open source under the MIT license, and live on GitHub Pages.

26
00:02:31,756 --> 00:02:34,828
Take a second look, before you act.
